OneiOS take20, userland/metal/patches/NativeMetal/0018: with oneios-prewarm=1, the render server keeps its render pipeline states, returns
the kept state for an equal descriptor at once, and makes the descriptors of earlier boots again 20 s after its first pipeline.
Upstream: none (OneiOS). Applies alone and with any of the others.
What: newRenderPipelineStateWithDescriptor:options:reflection:error: (every form of the call goes there) asks keptState() first and
keeps what it made (keepState()), only in backboardd and only with ONEIOS_PREWARM=1, never for a creation that asks for reflection
or with FailOnBinaryArchiveMiss. A state is kept under its descriptor: the functions' library content (length and an FNV-1a print
of the first and last 64 KiB), their names and constant values, every color attachment, the vertex descriptor, the depth/stencil
formats, the sample count, the topology and the raster flags (each function's constant words sorted); a descriptor with linked functions, preloaded libraries, tessellation
or vertex amplification is not kept. At most ONEIOS_PREWARM_MAX (1024) states. Each kept descriptor is appended once to
<cache>/prewarm.log in the journal's R-line form (written by a serial queue, off the render thread). 20 s after the render server's
first pipeline a utility-QoS thread reads prewarm.log and the journal (pipelines.log of a log-gpu=1 boot), rebuilds each descriptor
(functions from QuartzCore's default.metallib or the libraries the journal's L lines name, constant values from the line) and makes
it, one every 4 ms, pausing while the render server makes pipelines of its own; those states are kept, so the boot's own later
creations of the same descriptors are returned at once. A log over 1 MiB is written again with each descriptor once.
Why: take19's second session (-loggpu, the journal): of backboardd's 586 render pipeline creations, 26 compiled (25.0 ms mean, 47 ms
at most) and 560 found every artifact in the runtime's memo or its disk cache, and still took 1.3 ms (94, hit/miss/wr 0/0/0) to
4.5 ms (366, 1/1/0; up to 25 ms) each on the render thread, in bursts at each first use of a view after the boot (39 in 11 s at a
note's menu, 44 during the Home screen's lag, 55 for Maps): at 120 Hz, 4 ms is half a frame. The disk cache keeps the artifacts
across boots (take19's store: 52 entries read at boot, take18's 1670 kept), so a boot can make these pipelines before they are
asked for. NativeMetal 0017 keeps the clients' caches; this keeps the render server's states.
Risk: medium, hence off by default. A kept state shared by two callers (Metal pipeline states are immutable; Core Animation keeps
its own map of them). A descriptor field the key does not hold would make two descriptors look equal: the key holds every field the
journal's R line holds and the raster flags, and leaves out the descriptors with features it does not hold. The boot's extra work:
about 600 pipelines x 1-5 ms on a background thread 20 s after the start, and their GPU memory (shader binaries) for the boot.
Switch: boot argument oneios-prewarm=1 (ONEIOS_PREWARM=1, Switches.inc); oneios-prewarm-max=N (ONEIOS_PREWARM_MAX). Off without it:
nothing of this runs. Removable: yes (ONEIOS_METAL_SKIP=render-pipelines-kept-and-made-at-boot).
iOS's log (backboardd): `native-metal: prewarm: N of M descriptors made in S ms (K refused, D already kept)` once a boot, `native-metal:
kept states: H returned, C made` every 1000 returns; the A/B: the journal's creation count and times at the same views (log-gpu=1).
Checked on the PC: applies on b0d6a07 alone, after 0001-0017 and with each neighbour left out; the new section and the hooks pass
clang++ -fsyntax-only -fobjc-arc against stand-in declarations of the Foundation/Metal types it uses (those stand-ins are this
patch's own reading of the APIs: not Apple's headers). Read in the frontend: newLibraryWithFile: sets the library's bytes and
length (the key's print); a function's constantsText lists the indexed then the named constants in the constant values object's
own order (hence the sorted words). The CI is its first compile; nothing of it has run.
